# Reproducing {{ project_name }}

This guide starts from a fresh checkout and rebuilds declared outputs. The root
[`README.md`](README.md) Repository contract is authoritative for every path and
storage decision.

## 1. Create the environment

```bash
conda env create -f environment.yml -p ./conda_env
conda activate ./conda_env
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

For a byte-pinned environment, generate and commit `conda-lock.yml`, then use:

```bash
conda-lock install --prefix ./conda_env conda-lock.yml
```
{% if use_apptainer %}
For containerized/HPC execution, build only after a compatible lock exists:

```bash
apptainer build container.sif containers/apptainer.def
```
{% endif %}

## 2. Configure secrets locally

```bash
cp .env.example .env
```

Fill only required local values. `.env` is ignored; never commit it. Non-secret
scientific parameters belong in `conf/config.yaml`, dataset bindings in
`conf/catalog.yaml`, topology in `workflow/config.yaml`, and executor resources
in `workflow/profiles/`.

## 3. Fetch real data when configured

```bash
bash scripts/setup_data.sh
# dvc pull  # after the project has a configured remote and DVC pointers
```

Real `data/raw/` payloads are immutable and DVC-backed. Do not place synthetic
fixtures there; the optional example reads the small Git-tracked files under
`tests/fixtures/`.

For each newly approved real input, use per-file tracking and commit its metadata
before execution:

```bash
dvc add data/raw/<filename>
git add data/raw/<filename>.dvc data/raw/.gitignore metadata/<manifest>.csv
git commit -m "data: register raw input metadata"
```

The workflow rejects an untracked, modified, symlinked, or uncommitted real raw
file. Pushing DVC payloads remains a separate human-approved action.

## 4. Run the pipeline

```bash
snakemake --cores 1
{% if compute_backend == "slurm" %}# On the cluster:
snakemake --profile workflow/profiles/slurm
{% endif %}```

{% if include_example -%}
The bundled example validates `metadata/samples.example.csv`, produces per-unit
and cohort QC under `results/qc/`, writes the reusable analysis-ready dataset to
`data/processed/measurements.parquet`, renders light/dark figures under
`results/figures/`, and writes `results/provenance.json`.
{% else -%}
This render contains no example stage or fixtures. The default DAG validates and
resolves configuration, generates theme adapters, and writes current-run
`results/provenance.json`. It does not promise example QC, processed data,
figures, tables, reporting source, or a reporting Makefile.
{% endif %}

The pipeline may replace files in `data/interim/`, `data/processed/`, and
`results/`; never edit those outputs by hand.

## 5. Build reporting source

{% if include_example -%}
The optional reporting examples can be built with:

```bash
make -C reporting all
```

Article-like examples use the shared `lab.cls`; the Beamer deck uses its own
class while sharing fonts and generated theme colors.
{% else -%}
No example reporting source or `reporting/Makefile` exists. Build project-created
artifact source with its documented command.
{% endif %}

Working PDFs are disposable. A sent/presented artifact becomes durable only
through an explicitly approved immutable delivery.

## 6. Verify

```bash
snakemake --list
pytest -q
pre-commit run --all-files
git status --short
```

Verify each created/generated file against the marked root Repository contract:
generated payloads must be ignored, DVC payloads must have trackable pointers,
raw bytes must be unchanged, and no cache or notebook output may be staged.

## 7. Register a run only after human approval

`results/provenance.json` is current generated state and does not require
registration. If a person decides the execution is scientifically durable:

1. Commit every identity-bearing code, configuration, manifest, environment,
   and DVC metadata file.
2. Rerun the pipeline at that commit so current provenance records the same
   `HEAD` and bytes.
3. Register only after reviewing the generated manifest.

```bash
python scripts/register_run.py "short factual note"
python scripts/lab_notebook_entry.py >> reporting/writeups/LAB_NOTEBOOK.md
```

Registration appends `metadata/runs.csv` and archives the complete manifest at
`metadata/provenance/<execution_id>.json`. A person then completes the notebook
entry's interpretation. Do not run these durable mutations without explicit
approval.

## 8. Freeze an external delivery only after human approval

```bash
python scripts/freeze_delivery.py \
  --type <papers|posters|presentations|writeups> \
  --artifact <slug> --milestone <slug> \
  --payload <built-file> --figure <figure-file>
```

The execution must already be registered. The command creates a new immutable
`reporting/<type>/<artifact>/deliveries/<date>-<milestone>/`; it never overwrites
an existing delivery. See [`reporting/DELIVERIES.md`](reporting/DELIVERIES.md).
